  "slug": "early-topic-9f1e",
  "summary": "Quantitative 3D imaging of dorsal forebrain spheroids and organoid rosette studies now connect tissue architecture to progenitor regulatory programs.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is how signaling and epithelial architecture organize neural progenitors during early brain development. A SOX9-high radial glia program is reported to link progenitor maintenance to neuronal identity in cortical development, regulated by Notch and SOX9. Conditional loss of the Notch effector Rbpj in mice disrupts ventricular zone integrity and polarity, obstructing CSF flow and causing neonatal hydrocephalus. In human cortical organoids, neural rosettes are shown to arise from an interconnected tubular network formed by neuroepithelial folding and internalization. A whole-mount 3D imaging platform for dorsal forebrain spheroids detects altered growth and rosette phenotypes. The zebrafish meninges study, which implicates Foxc1 and Wnt, lies adjacent to this thread. What is new is the convergence of quantitative 3D tissue-scale analysis with defined regulatory programs.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
